Original commit body from ffbfc7eab6bbee5f3d738dc7f673b7f9bd2ed4a2:

Addresses INV-bamij, second batch (after ruby and lua in #1234). A call in
a static/const initialiser or a LazyLock closure (rust), a top-level
closure or an initializer with no symbol (swift), a top-level val or class
property initialiser (kotlin), or an object's val (scala) emitted no calls
edge. The sweep fixtures showed NONE at every such line on dev.

Two shared helpers in analyze/base.py:
- file_anchor_symbol(): the make_file_id node, the anchor of last resort
  (python and php already anchor module-level code on it).
- enclosing_declared_symbol(): the nearest ancestor of given node types
  that has a symbol, by position.
Each analyzer's call arm now takes: its function, else the enclosing type
(swift class/protocol, kotlin class/object/companion, scala
object/class/trait/enum, ruby class/module), else the file. Ruby and lua
move onto the helpers.

test_kotlin.py's test_call_at_top_level_outside_function_no_edge pinned the
drop as a design choice ("should not be attributed"). It is rewritten: the
call is still attributed to no function, it is anchored on the file.

